// Top 20 Must-Know Interview Questions (appear in 90% of bank QA interviews)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
const TOP_20_QUESTIONS = [
  { id: 'TOP-01', question: 'What is the difference between smoke testing and sanity testing?', answer: 'Smoke testing is a broad, shallow test to verify the build is stable enough for further testing — like checking if the application launches, login works, and major modules load. Sanity testing is a narrow, deep test on specific functionality after a minor change — like verifying that a fixed fund transfer bug works with NEFT, RTGS, and IMPS. In banking, smoke tests run on every CBS build; sanity tests run after hotfixes.', difficulty: 'Easy', category: 'Concept' },
  { id: 'TOP-02', question: 'What is your test approach for a new banking module?', answer: '1) Understand requirements from BRD/FSD 2) Identify scenarios (happy path, negative, boundary, regulatory) 3) Create RTM (Requirement Traceability Matrix) 4) Write test cases with banking data (account numbers, IFSC, amounts) 5) Setup test environment with mock CBS 6) Execute: smoke → functional → integration → regression 7) Track defects in JIRA 8) Generate test summary report 9) Get sign-off from stakeholders.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
  { id: 'TOP-03', question: 'Walk me through a critical defect you found in production.', answer: 'Structure: Situation → Action → Result. Example: "During UAT for NEFT module, I noticed transactions above Rs 2 lakh were silently failing without error messages. I raised a P1 defect with CBS team, traced it to a validation rule mismatch between frontend (2L limit) and backend (no limit). Fix was deployed in 4 hours. I added boundary value test cases for all transaction limits to prevent recurrence."', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Behavioral' },
  { id: 'TOP-04', question: 'Explain test plan vs test strategy.', answer: 'Test Strategy is organization-level, defines the overall testing approach (automation vs manual, tools, environments). Test Plan is project-level, details what to test, how, when, who, and entry/exit criteria. In banking: Strategy says "all payment modules need security testing with OWASP ZAP." Plan says "NEFT module: 150 test cases, 2 testers, UAT in Sprint 5, exit criteria: 0 P1 bugs."', difficulty: 'Easy', category: 'Concept' },
  { id: 'TOP-05', question: 'How would you test a fund transfer of Rs 1 crore?', answer: 'Test: 1) Verify daily transaction limit allows 1Cr (RTGS, not NEFT which has 10L limit) 2) Test with exact limit, limit-1, limit+1 (boundary) 3) Verify maker-checker approval workflow for high-value 4) Check that CBS debits sender and credits receiver atomically 5) Verify SMS/email notifications to both parties 6) Check audit trail and regulatory reporting (CTR for >10L) 7) Test rollback if receiver account is frozen/invalid 8) Verify GST on charges.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Scenario' },
  { id: 'TOP-06', question: 'What is boundary value analysis? Give a banking example.', answer: 'BVA tests values at the edges of valid ranges. For NEFT transfer (limit Rs 10,00,000): test Rs 0, Rs 1, Rs 9,99,999, Rs 10,00,000, Rs 10,00,001, negative amounts. For PIN entry (4-6 digits): test 3 digits, 4 digits, 6 digits, 7 digits. For account number (10-18 digits): test 9, 10, 18, 19 digit inputs. Always test min-1, min, min+1, max-1, max, max+1.', difficulty: 'Easy', category: 'Concept' },
  { id: 'TOP-07', question: 'How do you decide test priority with tight deadlines?', answer: 'Use risk-based testing: 1) Identify high-risk areas (payment flows, security, regulatory) 2) Map P0 scenarios first (fund transfer, login, balance inquiry) 3) Apply 80/20 rule — 20% of test cases cover 80% of user flows 4) Skip cosmetic/UI tests if time-constrained 5) Automate regression, focus manual effort on new features 6) Communicate risk to stakeholders: "We tested P0+P1 (85% coverage), P2 deferred to next sprint."', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Behavioral' },
  { id: 'TOP-08', question: 'What metrics do you track as a QA lead?', answer: 'Key metrics: 1) Defect Density (bugs per module) 2) Defect Leakage (bugs found in production vs testing) 3) Test Coverage (% requirements covered) 4) Test Execution Rate (planned vs actual) 5) Pass/Fail ratio per sprint 6) Mean Time to Detect (MTTD) and Fix (MTTF) 7) Automation coverage % 8) Requirements Traceability completeness. For banking: also track regulatory compliance test coverage and security vulnerability closure rate.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Technical' },
  { id: 'TOP-09', question: 'How do you handle flaky tests in automation?', answer: 'Strategies: 1) Add explicit waits instead of Thread.sleep (WebDriverWait in Selenium, cy.intercept in Cypress) 2) Isolate test data — each test creates its own data, no shared state 3) Retry mechanism (TestNG retry analyzer, Cypress retries config) 4) Quarantine flaky tests in separate suite 5) Root cause analysis — is it timing, data, or environment? 6) Fix the test OR the application. In banking: flaky tests on payment flows are dangerous — false passes could miss real bugs.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
  { id: 'TOP-10', question: 'Explain your CI/CD testing strategy.', answer: 'Pipeline stages: 1) Code commit → lint + unit tests (< 5 min) 2) Build → smoke tests (< 10 min) 3) Deploy to QA → API + integration tests (< 30 min) 4) Deploy to staging → regression + performance (< 2 hrs) 5) Manual UAT sign-off → production deploy 6) Production → smoke + synthetic monitoring. In banking: add security scan (OWASP ZAP) before staging, compliance check before production. Gate: zero P1/P2 defects to promote.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Technical' },
  { id: 'TOP-11', question: 'What is equivalence partitioning? Banking example?', answer: 'EP divides input into classes where values behave the same. For interest rate calculation: Savings (3.5-4%), FD < 1 year (5-6%), FD 1-3 years (6-7%), FD > 3 years (7-8%). Test one value from each partition instead of testing every possible rate. For account types: test one NRE, one NRO, one FCNR, one Regular Savings — each partition has different rules for TDS, repatriation, and minimum balance.', difficulty: 'Easy', category: 'Concept' },
  { id: 'TOP-12', question: 'How do you test APIs without a frontend?', answer: 'Tools: Postman, SoapUI (SOAP), curl, REST Assured (Java). Steps: 1) Import API spec (Swagger/OpenAPI/WSDL) 2) Test each endpoint: valid request → 200, invalid → 400, unauthorized → 401 3) Test request body validation (missing fields, wrong types, SQL injection in strings) 4) Test response schema matches spec 5) Test performance (response time < 2s) 6) Chain APIs for workflows: Create Account → Fund Transfer → Check Balance. Store assertions in collections for regression.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
  { id: 'TOP-13', question: 'What is the defect lifecycle?', answer: 'New → Assigned → Open → Fixed → Ready for Retest → Retest → Closed/Reopened. In banking context: New (tester finds NEFT failure) → Assigned (to CBS dev team) → Open (dev reproducing) → Fixed (code deployed to QA) → Retest (tester verifies fix + regression) → Closed (if pass) or Reopened (if fail). Additional states: Deferred (low priority, next release), Rejected (not a bug, by design), Duplicate. Track in JIRA with severity (Blocker/Critical/Major/Minor) and priority (P0-P3).', difficulty: 'Easy', category: 'Concept' },
  { id: 'TOP-14', question: 'How do you write a good test case?', answer: 'Structure: Test Case ID, Title (action + expected), Preconditions, Steps (numbered, specific), Test Data (exact values), Expected Result (measurable, not vague), Priority. Bad: "Test login." Good: "TC-LOGIN-003: Verify login fails with expired password. Pre: User account with password older than 90 days. Steps: 1) Navigate to login 2) Enter username: test_user_expired 3) Enter password: OldPass@123 4) Click Login. Expected: Error message \'Password expired. Please reset.\' displayed, user NOT logged in."', difficulty: 'Easy', category: 'Practical' },
  { id: 'TOP-15', question: 'What is regression testing and when do you do it?', answer: 'Regression testing verifies that new code changes have not broken existing functionality. When: 1) After every bug fix 2) After new feature addition 3) After environment changes (CBS upgrade, DB migration) 4) Before every release. In banking: regression suite includes all P0 scenarios — login, balance inquiry, fund transfer, statement generation. Automate 70%+ of regression. Run full suite nightly, smoke subset on every commit. Use RTM to identify impacted areas for selective regression.', difficulty: 'Easy', category: 'Concept' },
  { id: 'TOP-16', question: 'How do you test database/SQL in banking context?', answer: 'Test: 1) Data integrity — account balance in DB matches UI display 2) ACID properties — concurrent transfers don\'t corrupt balances 3) Stored procedures — interest calculation, EOD batch processing 4) Triggers — audit trail on balance changes 5) Indexes — query performance on large tables 6) Data migration — no data loss during CBS upgrades. Key queries: SELECT balance FROM accounts WHERE id=X (verify after transfer), COUNT(*) mismatches between source and target tables, JOIN between transaction and audit tables.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
  { id: 'TOP-17', question: 'What is the difference between severity and priority?', answer: 'Severity = technical impact (how bad is the bug). Priority = business urgency (how soon to fix). High Severity + High Priority: Fund transfer debits but doesn\'t credit — fix immediately. High Severity + Low Priority: Bulk statement generation crashes — bad but rarely used, fix next sprint. Low Severity + High Priority: Bank logo shows wrong color on homepage — cosmetic but CEO noticed, fix today. Low Severity + Low Priority: Help page typo — fix whenever.', difficulty: 'Easy', category: 'Concept' },
  { id: 'TOP-18', question: 'How do you test for security vulnerabilities in banking apps?', answer: 'OWASP Top 10 checks: 1) SQL injection — try \' OR 1=1-- in login/search fields 2) XSS — inject <script>alert(1)</script> in beneficiary name 3) CSRF — test if transfer form has anti-CSRF token 4) IDOR — change account_id in URL to access other accounts 5) Session — test timeout, cookie flags (HttpOnly, Secure) 6) Authentication — brute force protection, OTP bypass attempts 7) Authorization — test customer accessing admin endpoints. Tools: OWASP ZAP (automated), Burp Suite (manual). Run before every release per RBI mandate.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Technical' },
  { id: 'TOP-19', question: 'Explain STLC (Software Testing Life Cycle) phases.', answer: '1) Requirement Analysis — understand BRD, identify testable requirements 2) Test Planning — scope, approach, resources, schedule, tools 3) Test Case Development — write cases, create test data 4) Environment Setup — configure test environment, mock services 5) Test Execution — run tests, log defects, retest fixes 6) Test Closure — summary report, lessons learned, metrics. In banking: add regulatory review between planning and development, add security testing phase before closure, mandatory UAT sign-off with business stakeholders before production.', difficulty: 'Easy', category: 'Concept' },
  { id: 'TOP-20', question: 'How do you ensure quality in an Agile/Scrum banking project?', answer: 'Shift-left testing: 1) Participate in sprint planning — identify test scenarios during story refinement 2) Write acceptance criteria as Given/When/Then (BDD) 3) Automate within the sprint (not after) 4) Daily standups — flag test blockers early 5) Definition of Done includes: unit tests, integration tests, code review, security scan 6) Sprint demo — show test results to PO 7) Retrospective — improve test process. Banking-specific: regulatory stories need compliance team review, payment stories need end-to-end flow testing across CBS.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
];

